Why This Is Asked
- Tests dynamic augmentation of a trie with aggregated metrics and tie-breaking logic.

Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with subtreeSum and subtreeBest; O(length) updates; O(length) queries.
- Correct handling of weight deltas, insertions, and removals; lexicographic tiebreaks.

Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.subtreeSum = 0;
    this.bestWord = null;
    this.bestWeight = 0;
  }
}
class DynamicDict {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.wordWeights = new Map();
  }
  add(word, delta) {
    let node = this.root;
    const newWeight = (this.wordWeights.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    this.wordWeights.set(word, newWeight);
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.subtreeSum += delta;
      if (newWeight > node.bestWeight || 
          (newWeight === node.bestWeight && word < node.bestWord)) {
        node.bestWord = word;
        node.bestWeight = newWeight;
      }
    }
  }
  query(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        return [0, null];
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return [node.subtreeSum, node.bestWord];
  }
}
```

Complexity Analysis
- `add(word, delta)`: O(|word|) time, O(1) extra space per character
- `query(prefix)`: O(|prefix|) time, O(1) extra space

Edge Cases
- Negative deltas potentially removing words completely
- Multiple words with equal maximum weights requiring lexicographic tie-breaking
- Empty queries and non-existent prefixes
- Words sharing common prefixes affecting aggregate calculations

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests combining shortest-path DP on DAGs with path counting and reconstruction, using a topological order. It also touches large-number handling and edge case ties.

## Key Concepts
- DAG dynamic programming, topological sort, shortest paths in DAGs, path counting, reconstruction.

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, edges, s, t) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [])
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0)
  for (const e of edges) { const {u,v,w} = e; adj[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++ }
  const q = []
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (indeg[i]===0) q.push(i)
  const topo = []
  while (q.length) { const u = q.shift(); topo.push(u); for (const [v] of adj[u]) if (--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v) }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF); dist[s]=0
  const cnt = Array(n).fill(0); cnt[s] = 1
  const par = Array(n).fill(-1)
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const [v,w] of adj[u]) {
      const nd = dist[u] + w
      if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; cnt[v] = cnt[u]; par[v] = u }
      else if (nd === dist[v] && dist[u] !== INF) { cnt[v] += cnt[u]; if (par[v] === -1) par[v] = u }
    }
  }
  const path = []
  if (dist[t] !== INF) {
    for (let x = t; x !== -1; x = par[x]) path.push(x)
    path.reverse()
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: cnt[t], path }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple sources or multiple targets?
- How to handle very large counts with modulo while preserving reconstruction ability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model DP with obstacles and variable step sizes, requiring careful base cases and transition logic. It also assesses both memoization and tabulation familiarity.

## Key Concepts

- DP with multiple transitions (1..k)
- Handling blocked steps
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Time complexity: O(n*maxJump); Space: O(n) (or O(maxJump) with optimization)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Bottom-up tabulation
function countWays(n, maxJump, blocked) {
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) { dp[i] = 0; continue; }
    for (let j = 1; j <= maxJump; j++) {
      if (i - j >= 0) dp[i] += dp[i - j];
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

```javascript
// Memoized recursive version
function countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i = n, memo = {}) {
  if (i === 0) return 1;
  if (i < 0 || blocked[i]) return 0;
  if (memo[i] !== undefined) return memo[i];
  let ways = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= maxJump; t++) ways += countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i - t, memo);
  memo[i] = ways;
  return ways;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(maxJump) space with a sliding window?
- How would you adjust for modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow for large n?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests stability, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs between sorting algorithms. It asks for a concrete approach rather than a generic answer.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sorting
- Bottom-up merge sort
- Time/space complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSort(arr){
  const n = arr.length; 
  const aux = new Array(n);
  for (let width = 1; width < n; width *= 2){
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i += width * 2){
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, n);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, n);
      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right){
        if (arr[p].score <= arr[q].score) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
        else aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      for (let t = left; t < right; t++) arr[t] = aux[t];
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for memory-constrained environments where n is very large?
- Compare stability guarantees and real-time performance between mergesort and quicksort in streaming data.

## Why Asked
Tests understanding of graph traversal fundamentals, algorithm selection, and space-time tradeoffs in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
**BFS (Breadth-First Search)**: Uses queue, explores neighbors level-by-level. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(V) worst case (all nodes in queue). Guarantees shortest path in unweighted graphs.

**DFS (Depth-First Search)**: Uses stack or recursion, explores as deep as possible before backtracking. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(h) where h is maximum depth (recursion stack), O(V) worst case for skewed graphs.

## When to Use Each
**BFS**: Shortest path routing, social network friend suggestions, web crawlers finding closest pages, level-order tree traversals.

**DFS**: Topological sorting, cycle detection, maze solving, finding connected components, backtracking problems like Sudoku or N-Queens.

## Code Example
```javascript
// BFS - finds shortest path
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const distances = { [start]: 0 };

  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor);
        distances[neighbor] = distances[node] + 1;
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return distances;
}

// DFS - recursive implementation
function dfs(graph, node, visited = new Set(), result = []) {
  if (visited.has(node)) return result;

  visited.add(node);
  result.push(node);

  for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
    dfs(graph, neighbor, visited, result);
  }

  return result;
}

// DFS - iterative implementation
function dfsIterative(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (visited.has(node)) continue;

    visited.add(node);
    result.push(node);

    // Add neighbors to stack (reverse for consistent order)
    for (let i = graph[node].length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const neighbor = graph[node][i];
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        stack.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Memory Considerations
BFS can consume significant memory for wide graphs (queue holds all nodes at current level). DFS is memory-efficient for deep, narrow graphs but may cause stack overflow for very deep recursion. Choose based on graph structure and problem requirements.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of graph algorithms, state expansion techniques, and path reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm on expanded state space (node, used)
- Edge relaxation with and without discount application
- Path reconstruction across state transitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithDiscounts(n, edges, s, t, K) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>[]);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){ adj[u].push([v,w]); }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(null));

  const pq = new MinHeap();
  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.insert([s,0,0]); // [node, used, distance]

  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [u,used,d] = pq.extractMin();
    if(d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if(u === t) break;

    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      // Without discount
      if(d + w < dist[v][used]){
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used,d+w]);
      }
      // With discount if available
      if(used < K && d < dist[v][used+1]){
        dist[v][used+1] = d;
        prev[v][used+1] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used+1,d]);
      }
    }
  }

  // Find optimal state at target
  let minDist = INF, minUsed = -1;
  for(let k=0;k<=K;k++){
    if(dist[t][k] < minDist){
      minDist = dist[t][k];
      minUsed = k;
    }
  }

  // Path reconstruction
  const path = [];
  let curr = [t,minUsed];
  while(curr && prev[curr[0]][curr[1]]){
    path.unshift(curr[0]);
    curr = prev[curr[0]][curr[1]];
  }
  if(curr[0] === s) path.unshift(s);

  return {distance: minDist, path};
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Unreachable target: return INF distance
- K=0: reduces to standard Dijkstra
- K larger than path length: effectively unlimited discounts
- Multiple optimal states: choose minimal used count

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a practical data structure for real-world features (auto-complete) and how to extend it with per-word frequencies.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequency tracking
- DFS/BFS to collect candidate words from a prefix
- Sorting by frequency then lexicographic order
- Edge cases: missing prefix, zero matches, tie handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.freq = 0; // frequency for word ending here
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.freq += 1;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (n, path) => {
      if (n.freq > 0) results.push([path, n.freq]);
      for (const [ch, child] of n.next) dfs(child, path + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, prefix);
    results.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return results.slice(0, K).map(v => v[0]);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prune rarely-used branches to save memory?
- How would you support concurrent inserts and autocomplete in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to combine topological sorting with dynamic programming for shortest paths, avoiding Dijkstra's heap in DAGs. Uses O(n+m) time and O(n+m) memory and handles unreachable nodes.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort
- Relaxation in topo order
- DAG shortest-path correctness
- Edge cases: disconnected nodes, zero-weight edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function dagShortestPaths(n, edges, s){
  const adj = Array.from({length:n},()=>[]);
  for (const [u,v,w] of edges){ adj[u].push([v,w]); }
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){ indeg[v]++; }
  const q=[];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  const topo=[];
  for(let i=0;i<q.length;i++){
    const u=q[i];
    topo.push(u);
    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      if(--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const INF=1e15;
  const dist=Array(n).fill(INF);
  dist[s]=0;
  for(const u of topo){
    if(dist[u]===INF) continue;
    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      dist[v]=Math.min(dist[v], dist[u]+w);
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

